<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Sicily</h1><p class="st-meta">1071 CE – 1130 CE · capital Palermo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Norman county that conquered Muslim Sicily and built the multilingual, multi-faith government later inherited by the Kingdom of Sicily.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger de Hauteville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard's</a> youngest brother, crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1061 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>. With Guiscard he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, which fell in early 1072, and Roger became count of Sicily under his brother's overlordship. The conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily</a> took thirty years, ending with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noto-sicily">Noto</a> in 1091, the year Roger also raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Roger ruled a largely Muslim and Greek population with a thin Norman elite. He kept Arabic-speaking fiscal offices, recruited Muslim troops, refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> bishoprics and endowed Greek monasteries. In 1098 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> granted him unusual control over the Sicilian church. After his death in 1101, his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-del-vasto">Adelaide del Vasto</a> ruled as regent and moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a> came of age in 1112, absorbed the mainland <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a> in 1127 and was crowned king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on Christmas Day 1130. The county thus became the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-sicily.jpg" alt="County of Sicily" width="320" height="363"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_Italy_1112.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1071 CE – 1130 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Palermo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roger I of Sicily, Adelaide del Vasto, Roger II of Sicily</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, with Greek Orthodox Christianity and Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Greek, Latin, Old Norman French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>, <a href="/countries/malta">Malta</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Norman Sicily (county)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1100&amp;focus=county-of-sicily" aria-label="County of Sicily on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5013 808 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Sicily lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5333 1064L5333 1064M5333 1064L5383 971M5333 1064L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5333 1064h0M5383 971h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5333 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Palermo. <a href="/atlas?year=1100&amp;focus=county-of-sicily">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
